"""Hard questions and challenging teachings, added to the upper grades as one more unit each (two for young adults).
Each lesson gives what the Church teaches, why people struggle with it, how to answer with charity, and where to read the source."""
from spec import L, U

def Q(*rows):
    return list(rows)

EXTRA = {
'8': [U('Hard questions: faith, science and history', 'Church', 'Can I trust a Church that has made mistakes?', [
 L('Genesis, evolution and the big bang', 'Faith and science are both ways of seeking truth, so they cannot truly contradict each other. Genesis teaches why the world exists, not how it works.', '159,283,284,285,337,338', 'Genesis 1:1-2:3', 'creation faith-reason inspired', 'Does science disprove the Bible?', 'Write two columns: the questions science answers and the questions Genesis answers.', '',
   teach=['The Church teaches that God created everything and that faith and reason cannot contradict (CCC 159). The Church has not defined how God brought about living things; it teaches that the human soul is created directly by God (CCC 366).',
          'Catholics may accept the big bang and evolution. Fr. Georges Lemaître, a Catholic priest, proposed the big bang theory. Pope Pius XII (Humani Generis, 1950) and St John Paul II (1996) said evolution is not opposed to faith.',
          'The Church reads Genesis for its truths about God, creation, the goodness of the world, the dignity of man and the fall (CCC 289, 337, 390), as sacred writers used stories to teach.',
          'Not every Christian reads Genesis this way. Be kind to classmates who see it differently, and teach what the Church teaches.'],
   talk=['What questions does science answer best? What questions can it not answer?', 'Why might someone think faith and science must fight?', 'What do the first pages of Genesis teach about the world even if they are not a science book?'],
   misc=['“The Church says you cannot believe in evolution.”', '“Science has proved there is no God.”'],
   quiz=['Who proposed the big bang theory?|A Catholic priest, Georges Lemaître|Galileo|Charles Darwin|Pope Pius IX|0|He was a Belgian priest and physicist.', 'What does the Church teach about the human soul?|It is created directly by God|It evolves from animal souls|It is a product of the brain only|It is not part of the person|0|CCC 366.', 'Faith and science, rightly understood…|Cannot contradict each other|Must always be opposed|Are the same thing|Never speak of the same world|0|CCC 159.'],
   life='Ask a science teacher what question first made them curious. Pray a psalm of wonder (Psalm 8).', saint='Albert the Great: a Dominican who studied plants, animals and stars and taught that nature and faith belong together.', deeper=['apol:faith-reason'] if False else None),
 L('Galileo, the Inquisition and the Crusades', 'The Church is holy, but its members are sinners. The Church admits real wrongs and tries to tell the truth about its past.', '823,827,1428,1429', 'Matthew 13:24-30', 'church sin mercy', 'Can the Church be holy if its people do wrong?', 'Write a fair summary of one event from two points of view.', '',
   teach=['The Church is holy because of Christ and the Holy Spirit, but it is made of sinners, and it is always in need of purification (CCC 823, 827). Jesus said the weeds grow with the wheat until the harvest (Matthew 13).',
          'Galileo was tried in 1633 for teaching that the earth moves; in 1992 St John Paul II said the judges had made a mistake. The story is more complicated than “religion against science”: many church leaders and scientists were Catholics.',
          'The Crusades and the Inquisition included real violence and injustice. On March 12, 2000 St John Paul II asked forgiveness for the sins of the Church’s children, including violence in the service of truth.',
          'Be wary of both myths: “the Church has done nothing wrong” and “the Church has only done wrong.” Look at the sources.'],
   talk=['Why is it harder to say sorry for what a group did than for what I did?', 'What is the difference between the Church’s teaching and the actions of some of its members?', 'Why does the truth about the past matter?'],
   misc=['“The Church burned Galileo at the stake.”', '“If members of the Church did wrong, the Church cannot be true.”'],
   quiz=['In 1992 Pope John Paul II said about Galileo’s trial that…|The judges made a mistake|Galileo had been right about everything and the Church had never made an error|The Church should never admit errors|Galileo was a heretic|0|He recognized the error.', 'The Church is called holy because…|Christ and the Holy Spirit make it holy, though its members are sinners|Its members never sin|Its history has no failures|The pope is sinless|0|CCC 823, 827.', 'What did Pope John Paul II do on March 12, 2000?|Asked forgiveness for the sins of the Church’s children|Closed the Vatican Library|Canceled the Jubilee|Ended the Inquisition|0|The Day of Pardon.'],
   life='Read a short, fair article about one historical event and share one thing you learned.', saint='Bartolomé de las Casas: a priest who spoke out against the abuse of Native peoples in the Americas.'),
 L('Why does a good God allow suffering?', 'God does not will evil, yet allows it for a greater good. In Christ he enters suffering and brings life from it.', '309,310,311,312,313,314,1500,1501,1502', 'Job 38-42', 'free-will redemption hope', 'If God is good, why is there suffering?', 'Write a letter to someone who is hurting.', '',
   teach=['The Catechism asks the question plainly (CCC 309). There is no single answer; the whole of faith is the answer (CCC 309). God is not the author of evil, and he permits it only to bring a greater good (CCC 311–312).',
          'Some suffering comes from human freedom and sin, some from a world still unfinished (CCC 310). Jesus did not explain suffering away. He shared it, died, and rose.',
          'The right first response to someone suffering is love and presence, not an argument. Job’s friends were wrong when they explained too quickly.',
          'Christians are called to relieve suffering (works of mercy) and to unite their own to Christ (CCC 1521).'],
   talk=['Have you ever been helped by someone who just stayed beside you?', 'Why do arguments often fail a hurting person?', 'What does the cross tell us about God and suffering?'],
   misc=['“God sends suffering to punish us for specific sins.”', '“If God loved us, nothing bad would ever happen.”'],
   quiz=['Does God cause evil?|No, he permits it for a greater good|Yes, to teach lessons|He has no power over it|He does not know about it|0|CCC 311.', 'In the book of Job, the friends who explain Job’s suffering as punishment are…|Rebuked by God|Praised|Ignored|Healed|0|Job 42:7.', 'What does the Catechism say is the answer to the problem of evil?|The whole of the Christian faith|A single short argument|No answer exists|Only science|0|CCC 309.'],
   life='Visit, call or write to someone who is suffering this week.', saint='Josephine Bakhita: born into slavery in Sudan, she became a Canossian sister and said she would thank her captors because suffering led her to Christ.')])],
'9': [U('Hard questions about the Church', 'Church', 'Why does the Church teach what it teaches?', [
 L('Sinners in the Church: the abuse crisis and reform', 'The Church is holy and made of sinners. When leaders harm the vulnerable or hide it, that is a grave sin that must be faced with truth, justice and care for victims.', '823,827,1428,1431,2285', 'Luke 17:1-3', 'church sin justice mercy', 'How can the Church be holy when some leaders did terrible things?', 'List what a safe community looks like (rules, reporting, accountability, care for victims).', '',
   teach=['Sensitive topic: follow your school’s safe-environment policy and let students step out. Never ask anyone to share personal experiences.',
          'Jesus said it would be better for someone to have a millstone hung around their neck than to cause a little one to stumble (Luke 17:2; Matthew 18:6). Abuse of a minor is a grave sin and a crime. Hiding it makes it worse.',
          'The Church is holy through Christ, but its members, including clergy, are sinners (CCC 823, 827). In the United States the bishops adopted a Charter for the Protection of Children and Young People in 2002 and require safe-environment training and background checks. Reports and reforms continue.',
          'The right response is truth, justice, reporting to civil authorities, care for survivors, and reform. It is not leaving every vulnerable person without the Church’s care, nor pretending nothing happened.'],
   talk=['What makes a community safe for children?', 'Why does covering up a wrong often hurt people more than the original wrong?', 'How can people stay close to Christ when they are angry with leaders of the Church?'],
   misc=['“The Church has never dealt with the problem.”', '“A few bad priests means everything the Church teaches is false.”'],
   quiz=['What did the U.S. bishops adopt in 2002?|The Charter for the Protection of Children and Young People|A new Catechism|A ban on all youth programs|A new liturgy|0|It requires reporting and safe-environment training.', 'Jesus’ words about those who cause “little ones” to stumble show that…|Harming children is gravely wrong|It is minor|Only judges can speak of it|It should be kept private|0|Luke 17:2.', 'The Church is “holy” because…|Christ makes it holy, even with sinful members|Every member is perfect|It has never failed|Its leaders are sinless|0|CCC 823.'],
   life='Learn who to tell if you or a friend is being hurt, and pray for survivors of abuse.', saint='Josephine Bakhita: a survivor of slavery and violence who became a sister, patron of survivors of trafficking.'),
 L('Why can’t women be priests?', 'The Church teaches that only baptized men are ordained priests, following Christ’s choice of the Twelve, and that this does not mean women are less valuable. Women have always been central to the Church.', '1577,1578,1546,1547,1548,2334', 'Luke 6:12-16', 'holy-orders priest dignity', 'Is this fair?', 'List the roles of women in the Church, from Mary to the Doctors of the Church.', '',
   teach=['The Catechism teaches that “only a baptized man validly receives sacred ordination” (CCC 1577), because Christ chose men as apostles and the Church has no authority to change it (Ordinatio Sacerdotalis, St John Paul II, 1994).',
          'The Church does not say women are less than men. Equal in dignity (CCC 2334), the sexes have different callings. Mary, the greatest of all the saints, was not a priest.',
          'Women have led the Church as saints, Doctors of the Church (Catherine of Siena, Teresa of Avila, Thérèse of Lisieux, Hildegard of Bingen), abbesses, theologians, missionaries and heads of hospitals and schools.',
          'Many sincerely disagree. Understand the teaching, then discuss respectfully. Some Christian communities have different practices, but the Catholic and Orthodox Churches do not ordain women priests.'],
   talk=['What does it mean that people can be equal in dignity and different in roles?', 'Which woman in the Church’s history do you admire most, and why?', 'How should we speak with someone who thinks the teaching is unjust?'],
   misc=['“The Church thinks women are inferior.”', '“Women have no real role in the Church.”'],
   quiz=['Which document restated that the Church cannot ordain women?|Ordinatio Sacerdotalis (1994)|Rerum Novarum|Humanae Vitae|Lumen Fidei|0|St John Paul II.', 'Who among these is a woman Doctor of the Church?|Teresa of Avila|Monica|Clare of Assisi|Rita|0|Doctors include Teresa of Avila, Catherine of Siena and Thérèse.', 'The Catechism says the sexes are…|Equal in dignity and different|Identical in every role|Unequal in dignity|Unrelated|0|CCC 2334.'],
   life='Learn the story of one woman Doctor of the Church and share it.', saint='Catherine of Siena: a lay Dominican who advised popes and was named Doctor of the Church.'),
 L('Celibacy: why don’t Catholic priests marry?', 'Priestly celibacy is a discipline, not a doctrine, and a gift of undivided love for Christ and the Church. In the Eastern Catholic Churches, married men can be ordained.', '1579,1580,1599,1618,1619,1620', 'Matthew 19:10-12', 'priest holy-orders consecrated-life', 'Why does the Latin Church ask this of priests?', 'Compare the reasons for a celibate priesthood with the reasons for a married one.', '',
   teach=['Celibacy is a discipline of the Latin Church, not a dogma (CCC 1579–1580). Eastern Catholic priests may be married, though bishops are celibate, and some married former Anglican or Episcopal clergy have been ordained Catholic priests.',
          'Jesus and St Paul both lived and praised chosen celibacy for the Kingdom (Matthew 19:12; 1 Corinthians 7). The Church sees it as total, undivided giving (CCC 1579).',
          'It has costs: loneliness, struggles and failures. The Church asks that men be well formed and supported. The question of whether the discipline should change is a legitimate one, but only the pope and the Church can change it.',
          'Don’t confuse celibacy with the abuse crisis. Most abuse happens in families and many institutions; the answer is accountability and formation.'],
   talk=['What might someone gain and lose in giving up marriage for a mission?', 'Why would the Church treat this as a discipline rather than a doctrine?', 'How can we support the priests we know?'],
   misc=['“Celibacy is a Church dogma that can never change.”', '“Celibacy is why priests commit abuse.”'],
   quiz=['Is priestly celibacy a dogma?|No, it is a discipline|Yes, an infallible doctrine|It is a sacrament|It is a law of the Gospels|0|CCC 1579.', 'Which Catholics may ordain married men as priests?|Eastern Catholic Churches|None|Only monks|Only the Latin Church|0|Eastern practice.', 'Jesus spoke of those who “make themselves eunuchs” in…|Matthew 19:12|John 3:16|Luke 1:28|Acts 2:38|0|Chosen celibacy for the Kingdom.'],
   life='Write a thank-you note to a priest or religious who has helped you.', saint='John Vianney: the Curé of Ars, a priest who gave everything to his parish and heard confessions up to sixteen hours a day.')])],
'10': [U('Hard questions: life, love and the body', 'Morality', 'What does the Church teach about life and love, and why?', [
 L('Abortion and the dignity of the unborn', 'Every human life is a gift from conception to natural death, so direct abortion is gravely wrong. The Church also calls us to love and help mothers and children.', '2270,2271,2272,2273,2274,2275,2258', 'Psalm 139:13-16', 'sanctity-of-life dignity abortion-teaching', 'How does the Church answer the hardest cases?', 'Research a local pregnancy-help center and what it offers.', '',
   teach=['Sensitive topic: some students have lived through it. Teach clearly, with compassion, and give them room to step out.',
          'Human life begins at conception and must be protected (CCC 2270). “Direct abortion… is gravely contrary to the moral law” (CCC 2271). The Church treats ending an unborn life as a very grave wrong, and the Church’s law attaches excommunication to those who procure it (CCC 2272), with the hope of mercy through Confession.',
          'The Church recognizes real fear, poverty, abandonment and difficult pregnancies, including after rape. Teaching that the child has dignity never means leaving the mother alone: support, adoption and care (CCC 2367).',
          'Treatments needed to save a mother’s life that unintentionally result in the death of her unborn child, for example for a cancer or an ectopic pregnancy, are not “direct abortion.” Healing after abortion is real: Project Rachel and Rachel’s Vineyard.'],
   talk=['What are the best ways a community can support a pregnant teenager?', 'Why does the Church link the dignity of the unborn with the dignity of the poor and the sick?', 'How can we speak about this without shaming people?'],
   misc=['“The Church cares about babies only before they are born.”', '“The Church cannot forgive someone who has had an abortion.”'],
   quiz=['What does CCC 2270 teach about human life?|It must be respected and protected from conception|Life begins at birth|Personhood is decided by law|It depends on age|0|CCC 2270.', 'The sacrament that offers forgiveness and healing after abortion is…|Reconciliation (Confession)|Anointing of the Sick only|Confirmation|Holy Orders|0|CCC 1446, 2272.', 'A treatment to save a mother’s life that indirectly results in the death of her unborn child is…|Not considered direct abortion|Always the same as abortion|Prohibited as any surgery|Required to be kept secret|0|The principle of double effect.'],
   life='Pray the Rosary for mothers and children, or collect supplies for a pregnancy-help center.', saint='Gianna Beretta Molla: a doctor and mother who chose to protect her unborn daughter at the risk of her own life.'),
 L('Contraception, NFP and Humanae Vitae', 'The Church teaches that married love is meant to be total and open to life, so it asks couples to avoid artificial contraception and to practice natural family planning when they have serious reasons to space births.', '2366,2367,2368,2369,2370,2371,1652', 'Genesis 1:28', 'marriage-bond natural-law dignity', 'Why does the Church say no to the pill?', 'Read an excerpt from Humanae Vitae 12 and 14 with a partner and summarize in your own words.', '',
   teach=['Sensitive topic: aim for clarity and respect; this is a teaching many Catholics reject or have never heard.',
          'The Catechism teaches that the marital act has two meanings, unitive and procreative, which should not be separated (CCC 2366; Humanae Vitae 12, Paul VI, 1968). Contraception is therefore not permitted (CCC 2370).',
          'The Church also affirms responsible parenthood (CCC 2368). Spouses with serious reasons may use natural family planning, which uses the body’s natural signs, not drugs or devices.',
          'Not all hormonal treatments are always the same: medicines used to treat illness are a different question. Many couples find NFP demanding and some find it life-giving; the Church acknowledges hardship and offers support, mercy and Confession rather than condemnation.'],
   talk=['What do you think “total self-giving” means in a marriage?', 'Why might Paul VI’s teaching in 1968 have been so controversial?', 'What is the difference between NFP and contraception, according to the Church?'],
   misc=['“NFP is just Catholic birth control.”', '“The Church wants couples to have as many children as possible.”'],
   quiz=['Humanae Vitae was written by…|Pope Paul VI|Pope John XXIII|Pope Pius XII|Pope Francis|0|1968.', 'The two meanings of the marital act are…|Unitive and procreative|Legal and social|Physical and emotional only|Private and public|0|CCC 2366.', 'The Church allows couples with serious reasons to…|Space births using natural family planning|Use any method|Never plan families|Refuse marriage|0|CCC 2368, 2370.'],
   life='Pray for engaged and married couples you know.', saint='Zélie and Louis Martin: parents of St Thérèse and the first married couple canonized together.'),
 L('Chastity: what is it for?', 'Chastity is the virtue that lets us love well with our whole person. The Church teaches that sexual intimacy belongs to marriage and that every person is called to chastity in their state of life.', '2337,2338,2339,2340,2348,2349,2350,2351,2353,2354', '1 Corinthians 6:18-20', 'chastity virtue dignity', 'Is chastity just about saying no?', 'Write a personal rule of life for friendships, screens and dating.', '',
   teach=['Chastity is about integrating sexuality in the person, not repressing it (CCC 2337). Everyone, married, single or celibate, is called to chastity (CCC 2348–2349).',
          'The Church teaches that sexual intimacy is for the permanent, faithful, fruitful bond of marriage and that fornication, pornography and lust fall short of love (CCC 2351–2356). Pornography does serious harm to the person and to relationships (CCC 2354).',
          'Teens face real pressure and shame. The Church offers a vision of dignity and a path of mercy: Confession, prayer, good friends, honesty, healthy boundaries. Failure is not final.',
          'Chastity is a virtue that grows with practice, like any other. It is not the same as shame or silence about the body.'],
   talk=['What pressures do teens face about love and the body?', 'Why does the Church say chastity is about love?', 'What habits help someone to grow in this virtue?'],
   misc=['“The Church thinks the body and sex are dirty.”', '“Chastity means never being close to anyone.”'],
   quiz=['Chastity is…|The virtue that integrates sexuality in the whole person|Another word for celibacy only|Ignoring feelings|A rule for teenagers only|0|CCC 2337.', 'Who is called to chastity?|Everyone, in their own state of life|Only the unmarried|Only priests|Only girls|0|CCC 2348.', 'The Church teaches that sexual intimacy belongs…|In marriage|In any loving relationship|Only after engagement|Nowhere|0|CCC 2360.'],
   life='Choose one habit this week that protects your heart, such as a screen boundary or an evening prayer.', saint='Maria Goretti: a young girl who defended her purity and forgave her attacker.')])],
'11': [U('Hard questions: marriage, family and suffering', 'Morality', 'How does the Church walk with people in difficult situations?', [
 L('Divorce and annulment', 'Marriage is a permanent bond. The Church does not recognize divorce as ending a valid marriage, but an annulment can declare that a valid marriage never existed, and divorced Catholics are still part of the Church.', '1601,1638,1639,1640,1649,1650,1651,2382,2383,2384', 'Mark 10:2-12', 'marriage-bond annulment matrimony', 'Is annulment just Catholic divorce?', 'Study the steps of an annulment and the questions a tribunal asks.', '',
   teach=['Jesus taught that what God has joined together no one should separate (Mark 10:9; CCC 2382). A valid, consummated sacramental marriage can be dissolved only by death (CCC 1638–1640).',
          'An annulment is a finding that the marriage was never valid, for example because of a lack of freedom, serious incapacity or intention (CCC 1629). It does not make children illegitimate and is not a verdict on blame.',
          'Divorced Catholics are not excommunicated. Those who remarry without an annulment cannot receive Communion, but the Church asks that they be welcomed, prayed for and helped (CCC 1651; Amoris Laetitia, 2016).',
          'Separation may be necessary for safety (CCC 1649), especially in cases of abuse. The Church never asks someone to stay in danger.'],
   talk=['What are the risks to a family when marriage is treated as temporary?', 'Why might someone feel judged by the Church after a divorce?', 'How can a parish accompany people in broken marriages?'],
   misc=['“An annulment is just a Catholic divorce.”', '“Divorced Catholics are excommunicated.”'],
   quiz=['What does an annulment declare?|That a valid marriage never existed|That a marriage has ended|That children are illegitimate|That one spouse is guilty|0|CCC 1629.', 'A divorced Catholic who has not remarried…|May receive the sacraments|Is excommunicated|May not attend Mass|Must leave the Church|0|CCC 1651.', 'When may spouses separate?|When necessary, such as for safety|Never|Only with a bishop’s permission|When they disagree|0|CCC 1649.'],
   life='Pray for a family you know that is struggling. Offer practical help.', saint='Monica: a mother who prayed for decades for her husband and her son Augustine.'),
 L('Same-sex attraction and the Church’s teaching', 'The Church teaches that every person has dignity and must be treated with respect, compassion and sensitivity, and that marriage is a lifelong union of one man and one woman. It calls people with same-sex attraction to chastity.', '2357,2358,2359,1601,2333', 'Genesis 1:27', 'dignity chastity marriage-bond', 'How can the Church say both “love everyone” and “marriage is man and woman”?', 'Write a short guide: how to talk with a friend who is gay and a Christian.', '',
   teach=['Sensitive topic: some students are living this. Teach clearly, charitably and without humiliating anyone; let students step out; never ask a student to “out” themselves.',
          'The Catechism says homosexual persons “must be accepted with respect, compassion, and sensitivity. Every sign of unjust discrimination in their regard should be avoided” (CCC 2358). Same-sex attraction itself is not a sin. Acts are held to be contrary to the natural law (CCC 2357), and persons are called to chastity (CCC 2359).',
          'Marriage, in Catholic teaching, is the lifelong union of a man and a woman, open to children (CCC 1601). The Church’s teaching does not allow it to bless same-sex marriage as a sacrament.',
          'Many people find this teaching very painful. A good response listens, does not mock or condemn, and affirms that every person is made in God’s image and loved by God. Bullying has no place in a Catholic school.'],
   talk=['What does it mean to “accept with respect, compassion and sensitivity”?', 'Why can disagreeing with someone’s choice still be done with love?', 'What would it take for a parish to feel safe for everyone?'],
   misc=['“The Church teaches that being gay is a sin.”', '“The Church hates gay people.”'],
   quiz=['What does CCC 2358 say about people with same-sex attraction?|They must be treated with respect, compassion and sensitivity|They should be avoided|They cannot be baptized|They are excommunicated|0|CCC 2358.', 'Is same-sex attraction itself a sin?|No, the Church distinguishes attraction from acts|Yes, always|Only for clergy|The Church has no teaching|0|CCC 2358–2359.', 'In Catholic teaching, marriage is…|A lifelong union of a man and a woman|Any loving relationship|A temporary contract|A matter of civil law only|0|CCC 1601.'],
   life='Ask yourself: have I ever laughed at a joke that hurt someone? Choose to stop and apologize if you need to.', saint='Philip Neri: a priest of joy and mercy who welcomed everyone, including those on the margins.'),
 L('Euthanasia, suicide and caring for the dying', 'Life is a gift to be received, so the Church rejects euthanasia and assisted suicide, yet it also does not require extraordinary treatments and strongly supports palliative care and accompaniment of the dying.', '2276,2277,2278,2279,2280,2281,2282,2283', 'John 11:1-44', 'sanctity-of-life dignity mercy', 'What is the difference between letting someone die and killing?', 'Interview a hospice worker or read about palliative care.', '',
   teach=['Sensitive topic: some students have lost a loved one to suicide or illness. Provide resources and use gentle language.',
          'Euthanasia, acts or omissions that by their nature or intention cause death to end suffering, is morally unacceptable (CCC 2277). The Church distinguishes this from stopping treatments that are burdensome or disproportionate (CCC 2278) and from pain relief even if it shortens life (CCC 2279).',
          'Suicide is gravely contrary to love of self and God (CCC 2280–2281), yet mental illness, fear and anguish may lessen responsibility, and “we should not despair of the eternal salvation of persons who have taken their own lives” (CCC 2282–2283).',
          'If you or someone you know is in crisis, tell a trusted adult and call or text 988 (in the U.S.). The Church’s answer to suffering is accompaniment and care.'],
   talk=['What is the difference between refusing a burdensome treatment and killing a patient?', 'Why does the Church say pain relief is good?', 'What can a community do for someone who is dying or who feels they are a burden?'],
   misc=['“The Church says every possible medical treatment must always be used.”', '“A person who dies by suicide is automatically lost forever.”'],
   quiz=['The Church teaches that euthanasia is…|Morally unacceptable|A matter of choice|Required in some cases|Only wrong for Catholics|0|CCC 2277.', 'Which of these is allowed?|Refusing disproportionate or burdensome treatment|Directly causing death to end suffering|Withholding food and water to cause death|Assisted suicide|0|CCC 2278.', 'CCC 2283 says…|We should not despair of the salvation of those who have taken their own lives|Such people are certainly lost|They may not be mentioned|Prayer for them is forbidden|0|God knows hearts.'],
   life='Make a card for someone in a nursing home or hospice, and know the number 988.', saint='Cicely Saunders: a Christian who founded the modern hospice movement.')])],
'12': [U('Hard questions: justice, science and salvation', 'Morality', 'How do I hold to the faith when the issues are difficult?', [
 L('The death penalty, war and peace', 'The Church teaches that the death penalty is “inadmissible” today because of the dignity of the person, and that war may only be a last resort and must meet strict conditions.', '2263,2264,2265,2266,2267,2307,2308,2309,2310,2311,2312', 'Matthew 5:38-45', 'dignity peace justice', 'Can killing ever be justified?', 'Hold a structured debate using the just war criteria.', '',
   teach=['In 2018 Pope Francis revised CCC 2267: the death penalty is “inadmissible” because it is an attack on the inviolability and dignity of the person. Earlier editions allowed it in very rare cases if it was the only way to protect lives. The Church works for abolition.',
          'Legitimate defense may require force (CCC 2263–2265) and bear grave responsibility. A war may only be just if: lasting, grave damage by an aggressor; all other means impractical; serious prospects of success; the use of arms does not produce greater evils (CCC 2309).',
          'Conscientious objection is respected and those who serve are to see themselves as servants of peace (CCC 2310–2311).',
          'Faithful Catholics argue about how to apply these principles. Study the criteria before taking a side.'],
   talk=['What does it mean that every life has dignity, even a murderer’s?', 'Is a “just war” possible in the age of nuclear weapons?', 'How should the justice owed to victims be balanced with mercy?'],
   misc=['“The Church has always taught the death penalty is wrong.”', '“Any war is justified if the government declares it.”'],
   quiz=['What did the 2018 revision of CCC 2267 say?|The death penalty is inadmissible|The death penalty is required|The Church takes no position|It is allowed whenever the state chooses|0|The revision.', 'Which is NOT one of the conditions for a just war?|A declaration by a popular vote|Lasting, grave damage by an aggressor|All other means impractical|Serious prospects of success|0|CCC 2309.', 'The Church’s view of conscientious objectors is that…|They deserve respect and a way to serve|They are always wrong|They must be punished|They are heretics|0|CCC 2311.'],
   life='Read a short Church document on peace or the death penalty and share one question.', saint='Franz Jägerstätter: an Austrian farmer who refused to serve in the Nazi army and was executed in 1943.'),
 L('IVF, cloning and bioethics', 'The Church teaches that children are a gift, not a product, and that science must serve the dignity of the person. It supports ethical treatments for infertility and rejects those that replace the marital act or destroy embryos.', '2373,2374,2375,2376,2377,2378,2379,2292,2293,2294', 'Psalm 127:3', 'dignity sanctity-of-life natural-law', 'Why does the Church say no to some fertility treatments?', 'Compare ethical and unethical approaches in a table.', '',
   teach=['The Church sympathizes deeply with couples who struggle with infertility (CCC 2374). It teaches that a child is a gift, not something owed or manufactured (CCC 2378).',
          'In vitro fertilization separates procreation from the marital act and usually involves the creation and loss of embryos, so the Church holds that it is not morally acceptable (CCC 2377; Donum Vitae, 1987). Cloning and the destruction of embryos for research are rejected (CCC 2275).',
          'Ethical options exist: restorative reproductive medicine, NFP-based fertility care, surgery and treatments that help the marital act, and also adoption and fostering and service (CCC 2379).',
          'Adult stem cells and many other research paths are morally acceptable and effective.'],
   talk=['Why is it harder for a society to say “no” to technology that is wanted by many?', 'How can the Church speak with compassion to couples who have struggled with infertility?', 'What questions should we ask whenever science offers a new power?'],
   misc=['“The Church is against all fertility treatments and all stem cell research.”', '“A child conceived through IVF is not a person.”'],
   quiz=['A child conceived through IVF is…|A human person with full dignity|Less than human|Not loved by the Church|Not baptizable|0|The Church welcomes every child.', 'Which research is considered ethical?|Adult stem cell research|Cloning human embryos|Destroying embryos for stem cells|None|0|CCC 2275.', 'The Catechism’s advice to childless couples includes…|Adoption and service to others|Giving up on family|Using any technology|Pretending there is no pain|0|CCC 2379.'],
   life='Pray for couples who are struggling to conceive or to adopt, and consider how you can support them.', saint='Gianna Beretta Molla: a doctor and mother, a patron of mothers and unborn children.'),
 L('Is there salvation outside the Church?', 'The Church teaches that salvation comes through Christ and his Church, yet that God can lead those who do not know the Gospel through no fault of their own to salvation. It does not say who is saved or lost.', '846,847,848,841,842,843,844,845,1257,1260', 'John 14:6', 'salvation church mission', 'Does the Church say non-Catholics go to hell?', 'Read Lumen Gentium 16 and write what it says in your own words.', '',
   teach=['“Outside the Church there is no salvation” means that all salvation comes from Christ through his Church (CCC 846). It does not mean everyone outside the visible Church is lost.',
          'Those who, through no fault of their own, do not know Christ or his Church but seek God and try to do his will can be saved (CCC 847; Lumen Gentium 16). The Church does not say how, but trusts in God’s mercy (CCC 1260).',
          'This is why mission is not optional: the Church has the fullness of the means of salvation, and love compels us to share the Gospel (CCC 848).',
          'The Church recognizes truth and holiness in other religions (CCC 843; Nostra Aetate) and respects each person’s conscience.'],
   talk=['If God can save people outside the Church, why does the Church send missionaries?', 'How is the Catholic view different from “all religions are the same”?', 'How should we speak about our friends who belong to other religions or none?'],
   misc=['“The Church teaches that all non-Catholics are going to hell.”', '“All religions are equally true, so it doesn’t matter.”'],
   quiz=['According to CCC 847, who can be saved?|Those who through no fault of their own do not know Christ but seek God|No one outside the Church|Only baptized Catholics|Only the Church’s leaders|0|CCC 847.', 'The Church’s teaching on mission is that…|The Gospel must be shared out of love|It is optional|It is forbidden|It is only for priests|0|CCC 848.', 'What does the Church say about other religions?|They may contain truth and holiness|They are worthless|They are identical to Christianity|They should be abolished|0|CCC 843.'],
   life='Have a respectful conversation with someone of a different faith, asking what matters most to them.', saint='Francis Xavier: a Jesuit missionary to India and Japan who lived among the people he served.')])],
'YA': [U('Hard questions about the Church', 'Church', 'Can I trust the Church’s authority and its story?', [
 L('Infallibility and the development of doctrine', 'The Church teaches that the Holy Spirit protects the Church’s teaching from error on faith and morals under certain conditions. Doctrine develops in understanding without changing in meaning.', '889,890,891,892,85,86,87,88,89,90,94,66', 'John 16:12-13', 'magisterium dogma doctrine tradition', 'Does the pope never make mistakes?', 'Trace one doctrine through time: the Trinity, the Eucharist, or the Assumption.', '',
   teach=['Infallibility does not mean the pope is sinless or right about everything. It means that when the pope speaks “ex cathedra” on faith or morals, or the bishops with him teach definitively, the Spirit protects the Church from error (CCC 891). Ex cathedra definitions are rare: 1854 (Immaculate Conception), 1950 (Assumption).',
          'The Church distinguishes levels of teaching: defined dogma, definitive teaching, and teaching to which we owe “religious submission of mind and will” (CCC 892). A pope’s opinion in an interview is not an ex cathedra definition.',
          'Doctrine develops (CCC 66; Newman, Essay on the Development of Christian Doctrine): the Church understands more deeply what was given once, as an acorn grows into an oak. Authentic development is not contradiction. Vincent of Lérins asked of a belief: has it been held everywhere, always, by all?',
          'This does raise hard cases, such as religious liberty, usury, slavery, and the death penalty. Study each one honestly to see what changed (the application, the understanding) and what did not (the principle).'],
   talk=['What is the difference between a pope’s opinion and a definition?', 'Why would God protect the Church’s teaching at all?', 'How can a teaching “develop” without being changed?'],
   misc=['“Infallibility means the pope is never wrong.”', '“If doctrine develops, anything can change.”'],
   quiz=['Which of these was defined ex cathedra?|The Assumption of Mary (1950)|The papal states|The use of Latin|The date of Easter|0|Munificentissimus Deus.', 'Infallibility means that the pope is…|Protected from error when teaching definitively on faith or morals|Sinless|Always right on politics|Never to be questioned in anything|0|CCC 891.', 'Which best describes authentic development of doctrine?|Deeper understanding of what was always taught|Replacing teaching with its opposite|Dropping what is unpopular|Inventing new revelation|0|CCC 66, 94.'],
   life='Read a short account of how the Church came to define the Trinity or the divinity of Christ.', saint='John Henry Newman: an Anglican who studied history, wrote on development of doctrine and became a Catholic cardinal.', mem='Whoever listens to you listens to me. (Luke 10:16)'),
 L('The Church’s history: slavery, colonialism and anti-Judaism', 'Members of the Church have sometimes sinned gravely, including by supporting slavery, colonialism and hatred of the Jewish people, while popes and saints also spoke against these wrongs. The Church teaches that Jews are not collectively guilty of Christ’s death.', '597,598,839,840,841,2414,1928,2477', 'Galatians 3:28', 'church sin justice dignity', 'How should Catholics tell the truth about the Church’s past?', 'Read two sources, one critical and one defending the Church, and list what each gets right.', '',
   teach=['The Church teaches that Jesus’ passion cannot be blamed on all Jews then or now; “the Jews should not be spoken of as rejected or accursed” (CCC 597–598; Nostra Aetate 4, 1965). Centuries of hatred in Christian lands, which contributed to suffering and to the climate of the Holocaust, were sins against this teaching.',
          'Slavery is condemned: “the enslavement of human beings… is a sin” (CCC 2414). Popes condemned the slave trade and the enslavement of Native peoples (Paul III, Sublimis Deus 1537; Gregory XVI, In supremo 1839), yet many Catholics, including clergy and institutions, owned slaves or profited from the trade and from colonial conquest.',
          'On March 12, 2000 St John Paul II led the Church in asking pardon for the sins of its members, and Pope Francis apologized in Canada in 2022 for the Church’s role in residential schools for Indigenous children.',
          'A mature faith tells the truth about both the sins and the heroes (Las Casas, Peter Claver, Katharine Drexel, those who sheltered Jews in WWII) without either hiding or using the story as a weapon.'],
   talk=['Why is it harder to apologize for what the Church has done than for what I have done?', 'What does an honest examination of the Church’s history ask of people today?', 'Why do both Church leaders and ordinary Catholics appear in both the good and the bad of this story?'],
   misc=['“The Church has always condemned slavery clearly and no Catholic ever took part.”', '“The Church’s teaching has been anti-Jewish.”'],
   quiz=['Nostra Aetate (1965) teaches that…|Jews as a people are not guilty of Christ’s death|The Jews are rejected by God|All religions are equal|Only Catholics can be saved|0|CCC 597–598.', 'In 1537 Pope Paul III declared that…|Native peoples are truly human and cannot be enslaved|The Americas belong to Europeans|Slavery is a good|Conversion by force is allowed|0|Sublimis Deus.', 'CCC 2414 says the enslavement of human beings is…|A sin|A matter of custom|Permitted in war|Not discussed|0|CCC 2414.'],
   life='Learn about Katharine Drexel or Peter Claver and one act of justice in your own community.', saint='Peter Claver: a Jesuit who served enslaved Africans arriving in Cartagena and called himself “the slave of the slaves forever.”'),
 L('Mary: why do Catholics honor her?', 'Catholics honor Mary as Mother of God and the first disciple, and ask her to pray for us as we ask any friend. We do not worship her. The Immaculate Conception and Assumption show what God’s grace can do.', '490,491,492,493,495,966,969,971,2673,2674,2675,2676,2677,2096', 'Luke 1:26-56', 'mary immaculate-conception assumption intercession theotokos', 'Do Catholics worship Mary?', 'Find where each Marian title comes from in Scripture and the early Church.', '',
   teach=['Adoration belongs to God alone (CCC 2096). Honor given to Mary and the saints is veneration (CCC 971). Mary does not replace Christ; all her titles point to him.',
          'Theotokos, “Mother of God,” was defined at Ephesus (431) to protect belief that Jesus is truly God. The Immaculate Conception (CCC 490–493): Mary was preserved from original sin from the first moment, by the grace of Christ. The Assumption (CCC 966): at the end of her life she was taken body and soul into heavenly glory.',
          'Asking Mary’s intercession is like asking a friend to pray for you; the Body of Christ is one in heaven and on earth (CCC 956, 2683). The Hail Mary joins Gabriel’s greeting, Elizabeth’s, and a petition to pray for us.',
          'Protestant and Orthodox Christians differ on parts of this. Discuss the sources: Luke 1:28, 42, 48; John 19:26-27; Revelation 12.'],
   talk=['What is the difference between worship and veneration?', 'Why do many Christians find the Marian dogmas hard to accept?', 'How does Mary lead us to Jesus in your own experience?'],
   misc=['“Catholics think Mary is a goddess.”', '“The Immaculate Conception means Jesus was conceived without a father.”'],
   quiz=['The Immaculate Conception is the teaching that…|Mary was preserved from original sin from her conception|Jesus was conceived without a father|Mary never died|Mary is divine|0|CCC 491.', 'The Council of Ephesus (431) defined Mary as…|Theotokos, Mother of God|A goddess|A prophet only|An angel|0|It protected faith in Jesus’ divinity.', 'Catholics give adoration to…|God alone|God and Mary|Mary and the saints|The Church|0|CCC 2096.'],
   life='Pray the Rosary slowly with the Gospel scenes, and notice where Mary points.', saint='Maximilian Kolbe: a Franciscan devoted to Mary who gave his life for another prisoner at Auschwitz.')]),
 U('Hard questions about living the faith', 'Church', 'How do I hold the faith in a world that disagrees?', [
 L('When I disagree: conscience and obedience', 'Conscience is the voice of God in the heart, but it must be formed. Catholics are bound to follow a well-formed conscience and to give religious assent to the Church’s teaching.', '1776,1777,1778,1783,1784,1785,1790,1791,1792,2037,2039', 'Acts 5:29', 'conscience magisterium moral-law obedience', 'What should I do if I cannot accept a teaching?', 'Write what you do when you are not sure: a plan to read, pray, ask a wise person and listen.', '',
   teach=['Conscience is “a judgment of reason” by which we recognize the moral quality of an act (CCC 1778). We must always obey a certain judgment of conscience (CCC 1790), but it must be formed in light of the truth, the Word of God and the Church’s teaching (CCC 1783–1785).',
          'An erroneous conscience can be blameless if the ignorance is invincible, but we are responsible for forming it (CCC 1791–1793). Conscience is not just feelings or preference.',
          'The faithful owe “religious submission of mind and will” to the Church’s teaching authority (CCC 892), and some teachings are definitive. Struggle and doubt are not betrayal; faithful Catholics have wrestled with hard teachings (St Thomas More, Newman).',
          'Disagreeing in a way that slanders others or leads the weak astray is different from honest questions. Ask, study, pray, seek counsel and stay in the community.'],
   talk=['What is the difference between “my feelings” and “my conscience”?', 'How do we know when we are being honest about a hard teaching?', 'Who can help someone with a hard question?'],
   misc=['“Following my conscience means I can do whatever I feel is right.”', '“A good Catholic has no questions.”'],
   quiz=['An informed conscience is one that…|Has been formed by truth, Scripture and the Church’s teaching|Follows whatever feels right|Never changes|Ignores authority|0|CCC 1783–1785.', 'Must we always obey a certain judgment of conscience?|Yes, but we must form it well|No, never|Only in religious matters|Only when others agree|0|CCC 1790.', 'Who said “I die the king’s good servant, but God’s first”?|Thomas More|Augustine|Francis|Ignatius|0|He refused to violate conscience.'],
   life='Pick one teaching you struggle with. Read the Catechism passage and talk to a priest or mentor this month.', saint='Thomas More: Chancellor of England who refused to approve the king’s break with the Church and was executed in 1535.'),
 L('Faith, politics and the common good', 'Faith should shape how we take part in public life. The Church teaches principles on life, justice and the common good, but no party perfectly embodies them, so Catholics must form their conscience and take part with charity.', '1913,1914,1915,2238,2239,2240,2242,2244,2442,1905,1906,1907', 'Matthew 22:15-22', 'common-good social-teaching solidarity justice', 'How can a Catholic vote and take part in public life?', 'Compare a party platform with Catholic social teaching, listing agreements and disagreements.', '',
   teach=['Citizens have a duty to take part in public life, pay just taxes, vote, and defend their country (CCC 2240). The Church does not tell Catholics which party to join but teaches principles that apply to all of them: the dignity of the person, the common good, solidarity, subsidiarity (CCC 1905–1917).',
          'The Church’s social teaching includes protecting unborn children and the dying, caring for the poor and migrants, justice for workers, religious liberty, peace and care for creation (CCC 2237–2246; 2402; Laudato Si).',
          'Some issues involve intrinsic evils that can never be chosen, and others involve prudential judgment about the best way to serve the common good. Catholics may disagree about means. Faithful Citizenship by the U.S. bishops helps form conscience.',
          'Lay people have the main vocation in politics (CCC 899, 2442). We may not avoid it or use religion as a weapon.'],
   talk=['What do I do when no party fits Catholic teaching?', 'Which issues are non-negotiable and which are matters of prudence?', 'How do I talk about politics with family without damaging relationships?'],
   misc=['“The Church tells Catholics which party to vote for.”', '“Religion has no place in public life.”'],
   quiz=['According to Catholic teaching, citizens should…|Take part in public life and form their conscience|Stay out of politics|Always vote for one party|Obey every law without question|0|CCC 2240.', 'The “common good” is…|The conditions that allow everyone to flourish|The good of the majority only|Whatever the government says|Private interest|0|CCC 1906.', 'Which Church document by Pope Francis addresses care for creation?|Laudato Si’|Evangelium Vitae|Rerum Novarum|Humanae Vitae|0|2015.'],
   life='Read the U.S. bishops’ voting guide and discuss it with someone who sees things differently.', saint='Pier Giorgio Frassati: a young Italian layman who served the poor and took part in political life with courage.'),
 L('Doubt, dryness and the dark night', 'Doubt is part of many people’s journey. The Church distinguishes honest questions from willful rejection, and teaches that trust and prayer can carry us through seasons when God feels distant.', '156,157,158,162,164,2088,2089,2726,2727,2728,2729', 'Psalm 88', 'faith hope prayer', 'What do I do when I do not feel God?', 'Write your own “honest prayer,” as the psalmists did.', '',
   teach=['Faith is a gift and a free act that can be tested. “Involuntary doubt” does not destroy faith, but “voluntary doubt,” deliberately refusing to believe, is a sin against it (CCC 2088). Faith can grow through questions answered honestly (CCC 157–158).',
          'Prayer has dry seasons, distraction and acedia (CCC 2726–2728). The saints knew them: St Thérèse’s final illness, St John of the Cross’s “dark night,” Mother Teresa’s decades of felt absence.',
          'What helps: honest prayer (Psalm 88 ends in darkness and it is still Scripture), staying with the sacraments, a faithful routine, a spiritual director, community, service, and reading good answers to real objections.',
          'Doubt about God may also point to real suffering, depression or anxiety. Seek professional help, and never be ashamed of it.'],
   talk=['What is the difference between a question and a rejection?', 'What does Psalm 88 teach about honest prayer?', 'What helps you when you feel nothing in prayer?'],
   misc=['“If I have doubts, I have lost my faith.”', '“The saints always felt God close.”'],
   quiz=['What does the Catechism say about involuntary doubt?|It does not destroy faith|It is always a mortal sin|It means you were never a believer|It is the same as unbelief|0|CCC 2088.', 'Who wrote about “the dark night of the soul”?|John of the Cross|Augustine|Francis|Benedict|0|He wrote of spiritual dryness.', 'Which psalm ends in darkness yet is part of Scripture?|Psalm 88|Psalm 23|Psalm 100|Psalm 150|0|It shows honest prayer.'],
   life='Speak honestly to God for five minutes each day this week, in your own words.', saint='Thérèse of Lisieux: in her last illness she experienced darkness and still chose trust.')]),
],
}
